# POINT 2: Bastion Node bootstrap

## 1. Security rationale

The Bastion is the most sensitive machine in the lab. It will hold the SSH private key that administers the Target and the GPG key that signs your commits, so a compromise here compromises everything downstream. That drives these choices:

| Decision | Why |
|---|---|
| **Minimal toolset** | Every package is attack surface and something to patch. We install only what Module 1 needs. Ansible, Terraform and Docker come in later modules. |
| **ed25519 keys, passphrase-protected** | Modern, small, fast, and no weak-parameter pitfalls like RSA. A passphrase means a stolen key file is useless without it. |
| **`-a 100` KDF rounds** | Slows down offline brute force of the passphrase. |
| **One key per purpose** | The key for administering the Target is separate from the one for GitHub (Point 9). A leak of one doesn't expose the other. |
| **GPG: primary key + subkeys, 1-year expiry** | Expiry limits the damage of a forgotten or lost key. Subkeys can be rotated without changing your identity. |
| **Short agent cache lifetimes** | The passphrase is not held in memory indefinitely. |
| **Strict file permissions** | OpenSSH and GPG refuse or warn about loose permissions, and so do auditors. |

All of the following runs **inside the Bastion**, as `labadmin`, never as root. Keep the NAT adapter connected for the updates.

